volume: cow - Window Manager

# NAME

cow-module-cmd - internal commands used by cow modules

# DESCRIPTION

These commands form part of the interface between **cow**(1) and its modules.
They are documented for module authors and debugging, but are not intended as
general configuration commands.

# COMMANDS

## window-attach -t host [-s client] -n slot -x X -y Y -w width -h height [-v on|off] [-c on|off]

Attach a client to a named slot owned by a host window. Coordinates are
relative to the host's content origin and may be negative for an external
panel. The width and height describe the client's content area. CoW clips
clients to their slots and suppresses their server-side decorations.

Omit **-s** to update an occupied slot. Visibility defaults to **on**; **-v
off** hides the client without closing it. Clients follow their host's
position, desk, layer and iconified state. Repeating an unchanged request does
not schedule another layout.

**window-attach -t host -D** releases all attachments. Closing the host also
releases its clients, except those attached with **-c on**, which receive a
normal close request. The default is **-c off**. This policy survives CoW
restart and applies even if the host process is killed. Explicit **-D**
releases clients without closing them. Ordinary container editing commands do
not modify module-owned layouts.

## show diagnostics

Return read-only, process-lifetime diagnostic counters. The record contains CoW
process CPU, uptime and peak RSS; pending and cumulative update counts; manage
and render cycle counts; and last, average, and maximum callback and CoW/River
hand-off timings. Timing values are in microseconds.

The *request\_wait* measurement covers the first pending manage request to
River's *manage\_start* event. *after\_manage* covers CoW's *manage\_finish*
request to River's next *render\_start* event. These are protocol-boundary
timings, not compositor frame-rendering measurements.

The diagnostics have no *config* representation.

## window-tile \[-t target\] \[-e edges\] \[-u\]

Set or clear the tiled-edge hint for a window. This is used by modules which
place and size windows exactly, because some clients use the tiled state to
ignore normal resize increments.

**-t** *target*

: Target window.

**-e** *edges*

: Set the tiled edges. *edges* may be **all**, **none**, or any
  combination of *n*, *s*, *e*, and *w*. The default is **all**.

**-u**

: Clear the tiled-edge hint.

